**C1 — confirmed.** At `app/printing.py:17`, `job.pages // 2` rounds down, so a double-sided job with an odd page count is given one sheet too few. I checked the claim's example by hand. `jobs_that_fit([Job('a',1,1), Job('b',3,2)], 1, True)` sorts the jobs to `[b, a]`. Job `a` comes off first and costs 0 sheets, leaving 1. Then `b` costs 1 sheet (it really needs 2), leaving 0. Both jobs are returned, but they really need 3 sheets and only 1 was available. It should round up: `(job.pages + 1) // 2`. The function plans more paper than there is, so this is a real bug.

**C2 — confirmed.** The only double-sided test (`tests/test_printing.py:18-20`) uses jobs of 12 and 2 pages. Both are even, so rounding down and rounding up give the same answer, and the test passes despite the C1 bug. A 1-page or 3-page double-sided case would catch it.

